[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Everyone thinks making money with AI means building the next big app, but there are only four ways money actually shows up. And the easiest one you can start today for free from your phone. I built an audience of over 200,000 people'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and made multiple six figures from content with no team using this exact stuff. Here\'s the thing, making money with AI isn\'t about the tech, it\'s about who you put your work in front of. There'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are four ways to do that. They go from the easiest that anyone can start today all the way up to the hardest. By the end, you\'ll know exactly which one to start this week. The number one thing that decides if you make money is'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'traffic. Traffic just means how many people look at the thing you\'re selling. That\'s it. More people looking equals more money. Fewer people looking equals no money. It\'s that simple. So, you'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really need two things. You need something to sell and you need traffic [music] or people to sell it to. And those four ways we\'re going to climb, here\'s the secret. They\'re all just four ways to get traffic. Four different ways'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to get more eyeballs on your thing that you\'re selling. Your thing to sell can be a bunch of stuff. Here are the easy ones you can learn with Cloud Code in a weekend. One, you could build simple websites for local businesses. Two, you'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'could build small automations that save a business hours every week. Three, you make content and videos for businesses using AI. And four, you set up AI tools for people who are too busy to figure it'

[01:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'out themselves. So, pick one, just one. For the rest of this video, I\'ll use the first one. Building simple websites and booking systems for local businesses. Think a dentist booking page, a restaurant\'s online ordering, a'

[01:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'plumber\'s quote form, stuff that used to cost thousands of dollars and take weeks. That\'s your thing to sell and now you need traffic, so people who will look at it. So, where do you get it? You start at the bottom of the ladder. The'

[01:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'easiest traffic is there at level one. Level one is warm outreach. This is the bottom level, the easiest. Everyone should start here. Warm outreach is the easiest traffic on the planet. Why? Because it\'s people who already know,'

[02:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like, and trust you. Think your phone contacts, your old co-workers, your cousin who owns a gym, the guy you played soccer with, basically anyone whose email or phone number you have. You\'re not selling to strangers, you\'re'

[02:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'texting people who already trust you. So, you go through your contacts, you find anyone who owns a business or knows someone who does, and you send one simple message. Hey, I just started building websites and booking systems'

[02:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for local businesses. Do you know anyone who might be interested in this? That\'s it. No pitch, no pressure, and notice two things. You\'re not asking them to buy, you\'re asking who they'

[02:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'know, and you didn\'t say with AI, you said who it\'s for. People don\'t care how you build it, they care if it\'s for them. You just have to ask. But, here\'s the trap. Most people never ask. They feel weird about it, so they skip the'

[02:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'easiest traffic on the planet and try to jump straight to level four, which is the hardest one. Don\'t skip warm outreach. Your first customer is almost always someone you already know. So, that\'s level one unlocked. Warm'

[03:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'outreach, free, easy, do it today. But, here\'s the catch. You run out of warm people fast. You only have so many contacts. So, pretty soon you need traffic from people who don\'t know you yet. That\'s the next level up, and'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there\'s one trick that makes it way easier than it sounds. Let me show you. Real quick, all of this only works if you\'ve got a thing to sell. So, if you want a system you can build and start selling today, I made a whole video on building your own agentic operating'

[03:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system for content creation. Your whole little team of AI agents that runs your content. It\'s linked up in the corner, watch it after this one. Level two is cold outreach. It\'s one level up, it\'s a little harder, but it\'s still pretty'

[03:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simple. Cold means traffic from people who don\'t know you yet, so you got to reach out to them first. So, you find 10 local businesses with bad websites. This part\'s easy. Just pull up Google Maps and look up plumbers or dentists in your'

[03:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'town or use a scraping site like Apollo or Appify or even LinkedIn Sales Navigator. In 10 minutes, you\'ve got an entire list. And one of them is a dentist with a website from 2009. Now, here\'s the trick I promised. AI lets you'

[04:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'build the thing first before you even talk to them. You use Claude code to build them a better version of their website. Then you send one message. I noticed your site, I built you a free sample of a new one. Here\'s the link.'

[04:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Want it? You\'re not asking for a meeting, you\'re handing them a gift. And if you\'re wondering how you build something that actually looks good enough to win the client, I made a video on building beautiful 3D websites in just a couple of minutes. I\'ll link it'

[04:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'somewhere up here. That\'s the kind of free sample that gets you a yes. Just think of it like a free sample at the grocery store. You don\'t ask people to buy the cheese. You hand them a piece, if it\'s good, they buy. And here\'s the'

[04:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'best part, you can automate the whole thing. You can use Claude code to personalize each message and a tool like Instantly to send them out for you. So, instead of 10 businesses, you reach 100 while you sleep. The trap is people will'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'send boring messages like, "Hi, I do websites. Are you interested?" That gets ignored every time. Show, don\'t ask. Build the thing first. AI makes that almost free now and that\'s your edge. So, now you\'ve unlocked two levels, warm'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and cold. But, notice that both of them are you reaching out one person at a time. It\'s slow. So, here\'s the question that opens the next level. What if the traffic came to you instead while you sleep? That\'s exactly what happens next.'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Level three is organic content, another level up. And this is where it gets fun, but a little bit harder. Organic content just means you post stuff online for free. Videos, posts, tips, and people find you. But, here\'s the thing that'

[05:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'almost nobody gets. When you post content, you are letting the algorithm do your outreach for you. That\'s its entire job, to take your stuff and show it to the exact people who want to buy'

[05:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it for free. You stop knocking on doors, the algorithm knocks for you. So, instead of chasing businesses, you post what you do. Here\'s how I built a booking page for a dentist in one weekend. You show the screen, you show'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your result, and the algorithm hands it to the people who need it. But, here\'s the magic. With outreach, you get traffic one person at a time. With content, you get traffic from a thousand people on autopilot. Think of it like'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'planting a fruit tree. Outreach is picking one apple, content is planting the tree. It takes longer to grow, but then it feeds you over and over. Now, one thing to know is people don\'t buy the first time they see you. On average,'

[06:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'somebody needs to spend about seven hours with you or see seven pieces of your content before they buy. So, every post is a deposit, and they add up. The trap is that people quit at video three.'

[06:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They post twice, nobody claps for them, and they give up. But, here\'s the fix. Stop watching the likes. Watch your inputs, not your outcomes. You can\'t control how many views you get. You can control how many posts you make. So,'

[06:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'promise yourself one post a day, hit that, you won the day. The views come later. Focusing on the input is the thing that makes the grind easy. One more thing, and this is true for every level. While you\'re doing all of this,'

[07:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'talk to the people who buy. Ask them what they love and what they wished was better. Then, make your thing better every single time because the next level costs money. And you only want to spend money selling the best possible version'

[07:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of your thing. So, that\'s three levels unlocked: warm, cold, content. Content brings traffic to you for free, but it\'s slow. So, there\'s one level left, the top of the ladder, the hardest one, but also the fastest, and it\'s the only one'

[07:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'ve never touched until you\'ve done the first three. And here\'s why. Level four is the top of the ladder, it\'s the hardest one, and this is paid ads. Paid ads means you give Facebook or Google money and they put your thing in front'

[07:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of strangers fast. You are buying your traffic. So, you make one simple ad, I build websites and booking systems for local businesses. Want one? Click here. You put $20 behind it and strangers will'

[07:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'see it today. This is the fastest way to get traffic, but it\'s also the only one that costs real money up front. Think of it like a faucet. Turn it on, traffic flows, turn it off and it stops. It\'s'

[08:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fast, but you pay for every single drop. But here\'s the trap and it\'s the reason I made you climb in order because a lot of people will run ads before they\'ve ever sold a single thing. They burn $500 learning a lesson the first three levels'

[08:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'teach you for free. Don\'t buy traffic to find out if people want your thing. Buy traffic after you already know they do. Paid ads pours gas on a fire. You need a fire first. That\'s what levels one, two,'

[08:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and three build, the fire. Okay, step back, look at the whole ladder now. You can see all four. Warm outreach, cold outreach, organic content, paid ads. But if you look closer, they\'re all the'

[08:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exact same move. You have a thing to sell, you go get traffic. People look at your thing, some of them will buy it. The only difference is how you get the traffic and what it costs you. Warm outreach costs you a text, cold outreach'

[08:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'costs you 10 minutes, content costs you time, ads costs you money. And here\'s the part nobody tells you. You don\'t pick one, you climb them in order bottom to top. Start warm, then cold, then'

[09:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'content, then once you know people want your thing, pour gas on it with ads. Let me make this stupid simple. You need one thing to sell, pick from the list, a website, an automation, content, AI'

[09:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'setup, just pick one. You can\'t go wrong. Here\'s your one action this week. Just do level one. Open your phone, find five people you already know who own a business or who knows someone who does.'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Send them one message. That\'s it. Don\'t run an ad, don\'t film a video, just send five texts. That\'s your first dollar, I promise. So, if you\'ve been building'

